UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|EW can definitely make a diamond partial or 5@D, and probably 3N if they duck spades twice since I have the @DA and only started with 3 spades. Therefore, 3@S down 1 should be a reasonably good board for NS.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As usual, preempts make it hard for the opponents to bid, as this hand illustrates. Partner of pre-empter often has a stronger hand and bid 3@S, in this case for example, so it is hard for West to know what to do over 3@S. |
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is always a guess. I suppose East could balance with 4@D, but I am not sure that is correct. Balancing here may work out badly if it talks us into a making 4@S. |
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is always ^*Bproblematical^*n to bid to 4 of a minor in a competitive auction when the opponents have a major suit fit unless you are sure you are beating 4 of their major. |
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|And here with only 1 defensive trick, it is hard for East to know they are beating 4@S.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


