UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I would probably bid 3@C with the West hand, particularly not vulnerable, but it is slightly risky.|
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East passed over 2@S. But the singleton spade makes it very tempting to bid 3@C. Further, since partner bid 1N, denying a major, East knows that NS have a 9 card fit at least and West must have clubs in order to bid 1N.|
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The hand was well played. Had East bids 3@C I would bid 3@S. And if that is the auction, North should take the spade finesse rather than playing for the drop as he did earlier.|
PG| ***** 42 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Whenever one opponent has shown a long suit and you have a 9 card fit, it is more likely they are short in trumps. So ^*B8 ever 9 never^*n (meaning you always play for the drop of the queen in a 9-card fit) is not correct.|
PG| ***** 43 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If one opponent shows a long suit, the odds favor that you play the other opponent for the queen. In fact, even if there is no bidding, the odds for dropping the queen is only 52% vs 50% for the finesse, so they are essentially equivalent.|
PG| ***** 44 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Meaning if you always wanted to finesse for the queen with a 9-card fit, that is reasonable.|
PG| ***** 45 ***** |


